        "narrative_words": 257,
        "narrative": "The account received multiple large credit transactions between 2026-09-14 and 2026-09-17, totaling Rs.14.2L, from unknown remitters in Gulf countries, including Rs.175,000 on 2026-09-14 from an unknown remitter in Dubai, UAE, via SWIFT, and Rs.190,000 on the same day from an unknown remitter in Abu Dhabi, UAE, also via SWIFT. This pattern is inconsistent with the customer's declared profile as a delivery rider with a monthly income of Rs.22,000. The transactions trigger POE-002 (Business Profile) and POE-005 (Income Level), as the received amounts far exceed the customer's declared income. \n\nThe subsequent debits, including Rs.450,000 on 2026-09-18 via cash withdrawals at several ATMs and Rs.520,000 on 2026-09-21 via IMPS to an unidentified beneficiary account, further trigger POE-003 (Transaction History) and POE-009 (Transaction Size). The involvement of multiple unknown remitters and beneficiaries in these transactions triggers POE-007 (Beneficiary) and POE-010 (Transaction Complexity). The geographic locations of the transactions, including UAE, Qatar, and Saudi Arabia, trigger POE-011 (Geographies).\n\nThe record does not rule out the possibility that the customer has a legitimate, unreported source of income. However, it contradicts the customer's declared low-risk profile and income level, as evidenced by the large and frequent transactions. The pattern of transactions also triggers red-flag indicators, including the pass-through pattern of large credits followed by debits. \n\nThe statutory basis for this report is Section 12(1)(b) of the Prevention of Money Laundering Act (PMLA), which mandates reporting of transactions that may involve proceeds of crime. The transactions in question, due to their large amounts, unknown counterparties, and inconsistency with the customer's profile, necessitate further examination.",
